---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { loadContent } from "../../lib/content";
import { calBookingUrl } from "../../lib/site";

const { provider } = loadContent();
---

<Layout
  title={`The Making of a Global World | CBSE Class 10 History | ${provider.name}`}
  description="CBSE Class 10 History Chapter 3 notes on The Making of a Global World. Silk Routes, Conquest and Disease (Smallpox), 19th Century global economy, Rinderpest, Indentured Labour, Great Depression 1929, Bretton Woods, and an interactive 20-question board quiz."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Making of a Global World
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Social Science · History Chapter 3</span>
      <h1>The Making of a Global World</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Comprehensive revision notes on pre-modern global connections, the Silk Routes, colonial conquest through disease, 19th-century economic flows, the Great Depression, Bretton Woods twins, and an interactive 20-question board exam quiz.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Key Historical Concepts &amp; Chronology</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🧭 The Pre-Modern World &amp; Silk Routes</h3>
          <p class="formula">Ancient trade networks linking Asia, Europe &amp; Africa</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Silk Routes:</strong> Vibrant overland and maritime trade routes linking vast regions of Asia with Europe and northern Africa, flourishing from pre-Christian era up to the 15th century. Chinese silk, pottery, Indian textiles, and spices travelled west; gold and silver flowed from Europe to Asia.</li>
            <li>Buddhism, early Christian missionaries, and later Muslim preachers travelled along these routes.</li>
            <li><strong>Food Travels:</strong> Noodles travelled west from China to become spaghetti; Arab traders took pasta to 5th-century Sicily; discovery of the Americas introduced potatoes, soya, groundnuts, maize, tomatoes, and chilies to European diets.</li>
            <li><strong>Irish Potato Famine (1845–1849):</strong> Ireland's poor peasants became so dependent on potatoes that when disease destroyed the potato crop, hundreds of thousands died of starvation.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🦠 Conquest, Disease &amp; Trade</h3>
          <p class="formula">Biological warfare: Smallpox as the conqueror of the Americas</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>European sailors discovered the sea route to Asia and the Americas in the late 15th century (Columbus 1492).</li>
            <li>America's vast lands, crops, and minerals transformed global trade. Silver mines in Peru and Mexico financed European trade with Asia.</li>
            <li><strong>Smallpox:</strong> The Spanish conquerors' most powerful weapon was not conventional military firepower, but the deadly disease germs of <strong>smallpox</strong> they carried. Because of their long isolation, native Americans had no immunity; smallpox wiped out entire communities, paving the way for easy Spanish conquest.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🚢 The 19th-Century Global Economy (1815–1914)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Three Flows: Goods, Labour &amp; Capital</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Three Flows:</strong>
              <br/>1. <em>Flow of Trade:</em> Trade in goods like wheat and cloth.
              <br/>2. <em>Flow of Labour:</em> Migration of people looking for employment.
              <br/>3. <em>Flow of Capital:</em> Financial investments across long distances.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Abolition of Corn Laws:</strong> Under pressure from landed industrialists and urban dwellers, the British government abolished the Corn Laws (tariffs on imported corn), making imported food cheaper than locally grown food. Britain transformed into an industrial exporter.</li>
            <li><strong>Technology:</strong> Railways linked ports to agricultural hinterlands; refrigerated ships allowed meat to be transported from America and Australasia to Europe cheaply.</li>
            <li><strong>Rinderpest (Cattle Plague) in Africa (1890s):</strong> Killed 90% of African cattle, destroying African subsistence livelihoods and forcing local populations into European colonial wage-labour in mines and plantations.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🏛️ The Great Depression &amp; Bretton Woods (1929–1944)</h3>
          <p class="formula">From Wall Street Crash (1929) to Post-War Economic Order</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Great Depression (1929–mid 1930s):</strong> Caused by agricultural overproduction and the collapse of the US banking system. Catastrophic impact: factory closures, unemployment, bank failures. In India, agricultural prices crashed by 50%, jute prices collapsed by 60%, and farmers sold gold and silver ornaments to survive.</li>
            <li><strong>Henry Ford:</strong> Pioneered the mass assembly-line method for manufacturing automobiles (Model T Ford) in Detroit, drastically reducing manufacturing time and costs.</li>
            <li><strong>Bretton Woods Conference (July 1944):</strong> Held in New Hampshire, USA. Established:
              <br/>1. <strong>International Monetary Fund (IMF):</strong> To deal with external surpluses and deficits of member states.
              <br/>2. <strong>World Bank (IBRD):</strong> To finance post-war reconstruction.
            </li>
            <li>Known as the <strong>Bretton Woods twins</strong>. In response, developing countries organized the <strong>G-77</strong> group to demand a New International Economic Order (NIEO).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: The Three Global Flows &amp; Bretton Woods Framework</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 1 · The Three Flows of the 19th Century Economy</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Three flows of international economy">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Flow 1: Trade -->
              <rect x="20" y="30" width="420" height="55" rx="6" fill="#eff6ff" stroke="#3b82f6" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="35" y="55" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#1d4ed8">1. Flow of Trade (Goods)</text>
              <text x="35" y="73" font-size="10" fill="#1e3a8a">Largely in raw materials and manufactured goods: wheat, cotton, textiles, and refrigerated meat.</text>

              <!-- Flow 2: Labour -->
              <rect x="20" y="95" width="420" height="55" rx="6" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="35" y="120" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#b45309">2. Flow of Labour (Migration)</text>
              <text x="35" y="138" font-size="10" fill="#78350f">Migration of nearly 50 million people from Europe to America &amp; Australia, plus Indian indentured labour.</text>

              <!-- Flow 3: Capital -->
              <rect x="20" y="160" width="420" height="55" rx="6" fill="#ecfdf5" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="35" y="185" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#047857">3. Flow of Capital (Investment)</text>
              <text x="35" y="203" font-size="10" fill="#065f46">Short-term and long-term financial investments across oceans to build railways, mines, and ports.</text>

              <text x="230" y="238" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#64748b">All three flows were closely interconnected and deeply affected people's lives in the 19th century.</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="visual-caption">Economists identify three distinct global flows: trade in goods, migration of labour, and movement of capital.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 2 · The Bretton Woods Institutions &amp; G-77 Response</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Bretton woods twins and G-77">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Bretton Woods Center Box -->
              <rect x="130" y="15" width="200" height="35" rx="6" fill="#0f766e"/>
              <text x="230" y="37" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">Bretton Woods (1944, USA)</text>

              <!-- Branch lines -->
              <line x1="190" y1="50" x2="110" y2="85" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>
              <line x1="270" y1="50" x2="350" y2="85" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>

              <!-- Twin 1: IMF -->
              <rect x="30" y="85" width="160" height="60" rx="6" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#0284c7" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="110" y="107" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#0369a1">IMF</text>
              <text x="110" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#075985">Deal with external deficits</text>
              <text x="110" y="137" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#0369a1">&amp; trade surpluses</text>

              <!-- Twin 2: World Bank -->
              <rect x="270" y="85" width="160" height="60" rx="6" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#0284c7" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="350" y="107" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#0369a1">World Bank (IBRD)</text>
              <text x="350" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#075985">Finance post-war</text>
              <text x="350" y="137" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#0369a1">reconstruction &amp; growth</text>

              <!-- Counter Reaction: G-77 -->
              <rect x="50" y="165" width="360" height="65" rx="6" fill="#fef2f2" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="230" y="187" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#991b1b">Developing Nations' Response: Group of 77 (G-77)</text>
              <text x="230" y="205" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#7f1d1d">Demanded New International Economic Order (NIEO)</text>
              <text x="230" y="220" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#b91c1c">Real control over natural resources, fairer trade &amp; equitable development</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="visual-caption">The Bretton Woods twins aimed to secure global economic stability; developing nations formed G-77 to safeguard their economic sovereignty.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Interactive 20-Question Quiz -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Interactive CBSE Board Exam Quiz (20 Questions)</h2>
      <p class="muted">Test your concepts on global trade history, colonial migrations, and economic depressions with board exam standard MCQs.</p>

      <div class="quiz-container card" id="quiz-box">
        <div class="quiz-header-bar">
          <span class="quiz-counter" id="quiz-counter">Question 1 of 20</span>
          <span class="quiz-score" id="quiz-score">Score: 0 / 20</span>
        </div>

        <div class="quiz-question-body">
          <p class="quiz-q-text" id="quiz-question">Loading question...</p>
          <div class="quiz-options-list" id="quiz-options"></div>
          <div class="quiz-feedback" id="quiz-feedback"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="quiz-footer-actions">
          <button class="button secondary" id="quiz-prev-btn" disabled>← Previous</button>
          <button class="button" id="quiz-next-btn" disabled>Next Question →</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-completed-banner card" id="quiz-result-box" style="display:none; text-align:center; padding:2.5rem;">
        <h3>Quiz Completed! 🎉</h3>
        <p class="quiz-final-score" id="quiz-final-score" style="font-size:1.4rem; font-weight:700; color:var(--brand); margin:1rem 0;"></p>
        <p id="quiz-final-msg" class="muted"></p>
        <button class="button" id="quiz-restart-btn" style="margin-top:1rem;">Retake Quiz</button>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="cta-banner">
      <h2>Need personalized guidance for Class 10 Social Science?</h2>
      <p>Book a free {provider.trial.duration_minutes}-minute trial session with {provider.tutor_name} (online or at-home) to master answer-writing frameworks and score 100% in CBSE Board Exams.</p>
      <a class="button" href={calBookingUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Book Free Trial</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
(function() {
  const quizData = [
    {
      q: "The Silk Routes were ancient trade pathways connecting Asia with which other continents?",
      options: ["Europe and northern Africa", "North and South America", "Australia and Antarctica", "Only within East Asia"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "The Silk Routes were vibrant trade networks that knit together vast regions of Asia and linked Asia with Europe and northern Africa."
    },
    {
      q: "Which biological disease acted as the deadliest conqueror for the Spanish troops in America?",
      options: ["Cholera", "Smallpox", "Plague", "Malaria"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Smallpox germs carried by Spanish conquerors proved to be a deadly biological weapon because native Americans had no prior immunity against it."
    },
    {
      q: "The Irish Potato Famine occurred during which period?",
      options: ["1830–1835", "1845–1849", "1860–1864", "1890–1895"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "During the Great Irish Potato Famine (1845–1849), around ten lakh people died of starvation in Ireland after a fungal blight destroyed the potato crop."
    },
    {
      q: "What were the 'Corn Laws' in Britain designed to do?",
      options: ["Subsidize farmers growing corn", "Restrict the import of foreign corn to protect local British landlords", "Encourage corn export to colonies", "Distribute free food to factory workers"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Under pressure from landed groups, the British government passed Corn Laws to restrict the import of corn to maintain high domestic grain prices."
    },
    {
      q: "Which technological innovation made the long-distance transport of perishable meat possible from America and Australia to Europe?",
      options: ["Steam engines", "Refrigerated ships", "Diesel locomotives", "Air transport"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "The development of refrigerated ships in the late 1870s enabled transport of perishable foods like meat over long distances at reduced freight costs."
    },
    {
      q: "In 1885, the big European powers met in which city to carve up the continent of Africa among themselves?",
      options: ["London", "Paris", "Berlin", "Vienna"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "In 1885, the major European powers met in Berlin (the Berlin Conference) to delineate boundaries and carve up Africa into colonial possessions."
    },
    {
      q: "Rinderpest, which arrived in Africa in the late 1880s, was a devastating:",
      options: ["Crop blight affecting maize", "Cattle plague that killed over 90% of cattle", "Human disease like cholera", "Pest attacking rubber trees"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Rinderpest was a fast-spreading cattle plague that killed 90% of African cattle, destroying the pastoral economy and forcing Africans into wage labour."
    },
    {
      q: "Indian indentured labourers were primarily recruited from which regions?",
      options: ["Punjab and Kashmir", "Present-day eastern UP, Bihar, central India, and Tamil Nadu", "Kerala and Goa", "Gujarat and Rajasthan"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Most Indian indentured workers came from the present-day regions of eastern Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, central India, and the dry districts of Tamil Nadu."
    },
    {
      q: "What was the Trinidad Muharram carnival transformed into by Indian indentured workers?",
      options: ["Hosay", "Chutney music", "Carnival of Venice", "Diwali Mela"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "In Trinidad, the annual Muharram procession was transformed into a riotous carnival called 'Hosay' in which workers of all races and religions participated."
    },
    {
      q: "Who pioneered the mass production assembly-line technique for automobiles in the United States?",
      options: ["John D. Rockefeller", "Henry Ford", "J.P. Morgan", "Thomas Edison"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Henry Ford adapted the assembly-line method of a Chicago slaughterhouse to his car plant in Detroit, producing the famous Model T Ford."
    },
    {
      q: "In which year did the Great Depression begin with the collapse of the US stock market?",
      options: ["1919", "1929", "1939", "1945"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "The Great Depression began around 1929 following the Wall Street Crash in the US and lasted until the mid-1930s."
    },
    {
      q: "What was the impact of the Great Depression on Indian agricultural exports?",
      options: ["Exports doubled", "Exports collapsed by approximately 50%", "There was zero impact", "India imported large amounts of food"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Between 1928 and 1934, India's exports and imports nearly halved as international prices plunged by 50%."
    },
    {
      q: "The Bretton Woods Conference of July 1944 was held in which US state?",
      options: ["New York", "California", "New Hampshire", "Massachusetts"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "The United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference was held in July 1944 at Bretton Woods in New Hampshire, USA."
    },
    {
      q: "Which two institutions are famously known as the 'Bretton Woods Twins'?",
      options: ["UNESCO and UNICEF", "IMF and the World Bank (IBRD)", "WTO and WHO", "Federal Reserve and Bank of England"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "The International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (World Bank) are known as the Bretton Woods twins."
    },
    {
      q: "What did the Group of 77 (G-77) developing countries demand to protect their economic interests?",
      options: ["A New International Economic Order (NIEO)", "Abolition of the United Nations", "Restoration of the gold standard", "Free trade with no environmental rules"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "Developing countries organized themselves as the G-77 to demand a New International Economic Order (NIEO) ensuring sovereignty over natural resources and fairer trade."
    },
    {
      q: "Which Indian banking and financial community financed export agriculture in Central and Southeast Asia using indigenous systems?",
      options: ["Shikaripuri shroffs and Nattukottai Chettiars", "Parsis of Bombay", "Birlas and Tatas", "Marwaris of Calcutta"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "Shikaripuri shroffs and Nattukottai Chettiars were among the prominent groups of Indian bankers and traders who financed export agriculture across Central and Southeast Asia."
    },
    {
      q: "During the Great Depression, what precious commodity did India export in large quantities to balance trade?",
      options: ["Silver", "Gold", "Diamonds", "Copper"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "During the Depression years, indebted Indian peasants sold their family jewelry and savings, turning India into a net exporter of precious metal, notably gold."
    },
    {
      q: "Which Nobel Prize-winning writer is a descendant of Indian indentured migrants in the Caribbean?",
      options: ["Salman Rushdie", "V.S. Naipaul", "Arundhati Roy", "Amitav Ghosh"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "The Nobel Prize-winning writer V.S. Naipaul is a descendant of Indian indentured labourers who migrated to Trinidad."
    },
    {
      q: "Before the 18th century, which two countries were among the world's richest and pre-eminent in Asian trade?",
      options: ["China and India", "Britain and France", "USA and Japan", "Germany and Russia"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "Until well into the eighteenth century, China and India were among the world's richest countries and dominated world trade."
    },
    {
      q: "What is an 'indentured labourer'?",
      options: ["A bonded labourer under contract to work for an employer for a specific amount of time", "A free industrial worker earning hourly wages", "A permanent slave with no legal rights", "A self-employed artisan"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "An indentured labourer is a bonded worker under a legal contract to work for an employer for a specific period of time to pay off passage expenses."
    }
  ];

  let currentIdx = 0;
  let userAnswers = new Array(quizData.length).fill(null);

  const qText = document.getElementById("quiz-question");
  const optionsBox = document.getElementById("quiz-options");
  const feedbackBox = document.getElementById("quiz-feedback");
  const counterBox = document.getElementById("quiz-counter");
  const scoreBox = document.getElementById("quiz-score");
  const prevBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-prev-btn");
  const nextBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-next-btn");
  const quizBox = document.getElementById("quiz-box");
  const resultBox = document.getElementById("quiz-result-box");
  const finalScore = document.getElementById("quiz-final-score");
  const finalMsg = document.getElementById("quiz-final-msg");
  const restartBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-restart-btn");

  function renderQuestion(idx) {
    const item = quizData[idx];
    counterBox.textContent = `Question ${idx + 1} of ${quizData.length}`;
    qText.textContent = item.q;
    optionsBox.innerHTML = "";
    feedbackBox.textContent = "";
    feedbackBox.className = "quiz-feedback";

    const selected = userAnswers[idx];

    item.options.forEach((optText, oIdx) => {
      const btn = document.createElement("button");
      btn.className = "quiz-opt-btn";
      btn.textContent = `${String.fromCharCode(65 + oIdx)}. ${optText}`;

      if (selected !== null) {
        btn.disabled = true;
        if (oIdx === item.answer) {
          btn.classList.add("opt-correct");
        } else if (oIdx === selected) {
          btn.classList.add("opt-wrong");
        }
      } else {
        btn.onclick = () => selectOption(oIdx);
      }

      optionsBox.appendChild(btn);
    });

    if (selected !== null) {
      if (selected === item.answer) {
        feedbackBox.textContent = `✓ Correct! ${item.explanation}`;
        feedbackBox.classList.add("feedback-correct");
      } else {
        feedbackBox.textContent = `✗ Incorrect. Correct answer is option ${String.fromCharCode(65 + item.answer)}. ${item.explanation}`;
        feedbackBox.classList.add("feedback-wrong");
      }
    }

    prevBtn.disabled = idx === 0;
    nextBtn.disabled = selected === null;
    nextBtn.textContent = idx === quizData.length - 1 ? "Finish Quiz →" : "Next Question →";
    updateScoreDisplay();
  }

  function selectOption(oIdx) {
    userAnswers[currentIdx] = oIdx;
    renderQuestion(currentIdx);
  }

  function updateScoreDisplay() {
    let score = 0;
    userAnswers.forEach((ans, i) => {
      if (ans === quizData[i].answer) score++;
    });
    scoreBox.textContent = `Score: ${score} / ${quizData.length}`;
  }

  prevBtn.onclick = () => {
    if (currentIdx > 0) {
      currentIdx--;
      renderQuestion(currentIdx);
    }
  };

  nextBtn.onclick = () => {
    if (currentIdx < quizData.length - 1) {
      currentIdx++;
      renderQuestion(currentIdx);
    } else {
      finishQuiz();
    }
  };

  function finishQuiz() {
    quizBox.style.display = "none";
    resultBox.style.display = "block";
    let score = 0;
    userAnswers.forEach((ans, i) => {
      if (ans === quizData[i].answer) score++;
    });
    finalScore.textContent = `Your Final Score: ${score} / ${quizData.length} (${Math.round((score / quizData.length) * 100)}%)`;
    if (score >= 18) {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Outstanding mastery! You have exceptional clarity on pre-modern trade, 19th-century economic flows, and Bretton Woods.";
    } else if (score >= 14) {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Great job! Review the Great Depression impact on Indian agriculture and Rinderpest consequences to aim for 100%.";
    } else {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Review the three flows, food travels, and institution profiles above, then retry!";
    }
  }

  restartBtn.onclick = () => {
    userAnswers = new Array(quizData.length).fill(null);
    currentIdx = 0;
    resultBox.style.display = "none";
    quizBox.style.display = "block";
    renderQuestion(0);
  };

  renderQuestion(0);
})();
</script>

<style>
  .resource-header {
    padding-block: 1.5rem 2rem;
  }

  .clean-list {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .clean-list li + li {
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
  }

  .formula {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--brand);
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0.6rem;
    font-family: inherit;
  }

  /* ── Visual Cards ── */
  .visual-card {
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .visual-badge {
    background: var(--paper);
    padding: 0.55rem 1rem;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--brand);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .svg-container {
    padding: 1rem;
    background: #f8fafc;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .svg-container svg {
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .visual-caption {
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem 1rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: #fff;
  }

  /* ── Quiz Section ── */
  .quiz-container {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .quiz-header-bar {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding-bottom: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--brand);
  }

  .quiz-q-text {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .quiz-options-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.6rem;
